Description: GtkScrollbar: scroll arrows a theme can turn on
 GTK 4 removed the steppers of GTK 3's scrollbars ("range: Remove steppers",
 7e525ca6), so a theme recalling a classic desktop cannot show scroll arrows
 in GTK 4 as it does in GTK 2 and GTK 3. The four steppers come back as
 children of the scrollbar, in GTK 3's order and with its names: a backward
 (stepper.up) and a secondary forward (stepper.down) stepper before the range,
 a secondary backward (stepper.up) and a forward (stepper.down) stepper after
 it. A stepper takes room only when the theme gives it a size, so a theme
 without stepper rules draws every scrollbar as before; the scrollbar lays its
 children out itself instead of a box layout, so a stepper without a size
 adds no spacing either. The behaviour is GTK 3's: the primary button moves
 by a step, the secondary one by a page, both going on while held (after GTK
 3's 500 ms, through the range's own autoscroll), the middle button moves to
 the start or the end, a stepper is insensitive while the slider is at the
 end it moves towards, and a horizontal range flipped in right-to-left text is
 followed. The scrollbar now follows the range's adjustment also when the
 range made one itself (no adjustment given), for the accessible value too.
Author: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Origin: other, Sparky Stereo OS (queue row 147, milestone 3)
